From: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: cavium: octeon_pip: prefetch the shared info and the page struct a packet ahead

EXPERIMENTAL, showcase only, not for merging: it needs proper testing.
It changes the octeon_pip driver and depends on its PR (#26060).
The build does not apply it: it sits in target/linux/octeon/experimental/.

The poll prefetches the next packet's work entry and its first data
line. The skb build then writes the skb_shared_info at the end of the
buffer and looks at the page struct, and the stack reads both back: the
L1 is write-through without allocation on write, so the first read of a
line written first misses. Prefetch both lines with the data, a packet
ahead.

Measured on an EdgeRouter 4 (CN7030, 4 cores), octeon3 subtarget (CN63XXP1
off), octeon_pip driver, one boot per image, 64-byte single-flow floods
in kpps, on 2026-10-21.
Target: napi_build_skb() 1.24 -> 0.11 L1 misses per packet (flood1).
Step oct3B -> P1 (077-080 together): flood1 +3.8 %, fw4 NAT +1.4 %,
fw4+flowtable +4.1 %, GRE tcp1 +7.9 %.
This patch, extrapolated from its share of the misses the step removed (1.13 of 2.65):
flood1 ~+1.6 %, fw4 NAT ~+0.6 %, fw4+flowtable ~+1.7 %, GRE ~+1.5 %.
These gains are on top of an unpatched octeon3 kernel; the full set and the
split gain (up to +15 %) are in target/linux/octeon/experimental/README.md.

Signed-off-by: Simon Polack <spolack+git@mailbox.org>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cavium/octeon/octeon_pip_rx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cavium/octeon/octeon_pip_rx.c
@@ -800,6 +800,23 @@ static unsigned int opip_rx_refill(struc
 	return n;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The lines the stack touches first, a packet ahead: the packet data (the
+ * headers), the skb_shared_info the skb build writes and the stack reads
+ * back, and the page struct the build and the free look at. The L1 is
+ * write-through without allocation on write: a line written first and
+ * read then misses on the read unless it is in by then.
+ */
+static __always_inline void opip_rx_prefetch_packet(const struct opip_wqe *wqe)
+{
+	void *buffer = opip_virt(opip_buf_start(wqe->packet_ptr));
+
+	prefetch(opip_virt(opip_buf_addr(wqe->packet_ptr)));
+	prefetch(buffer + OPIP_RX_FRAG_SIZE -
+		 SKB_DATA_ALIGN(sizeof(struct skb_shared_info)));
+	prefetch(virt_to_page(buffer));
+}
+
 static __always_inline int __opip_poll(struct opip_group *group, int budget,
 				       const bool xdp_on)
 {
@@ -881,7 +898,7 @@ static __always_inline int __opip_poll(s
 		}
 
 		/* The packet data, the stack reads its headers first */
-		prefetch(opip_virt(opip_buf_addr(wqe->packet_ptr)));
+		opip_rx_prefetch_packet(wqe);
 		rx_count++;
 
 		port = opip_rx_port(wqe, pknd);
@@ -910,9 +927,9 @@ static __always_inline int __opip_poll(s
 		if (!(rx_count & (OPIP_TX_POLL_TICK - 1)))
 			opip_tx_poll_tick(&group->tx_poll);
 
-		/* Its line is in by now: have the next packet's data follow */
+		/* Its line is in by now: have the next packet's lines follow */
 		if (next)
-			prefetch(opip_virt(opip_buf_addr(next->packet_ptr)));
+			opip_rx_prefetch_packet(next);
 		wqe = next;
 	}
 	if (idle)
